.\" bertillon(1) -- man-macro reference page
.\" $Revision: 1.1 $
.\" $Log: bertillon.1,v $
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/09/22 00:04:15  dlr
.\" initial. Documents all 15 option surfaces, twelve executed output blocks, the stdin/file asymmetry, and the limitations that read as bugs and are not. Clean under groff -man -ww and mandoc -Tlint.
.\"
.TH BERTILLON 1 "2026-09-21" "hashpipe 1.203" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
bertillon \- list the registered hash types that could have produced a value
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bertillon
.RI [ options "] [" target " ...]"
.br
.B bertillon
.BR \-g | \-\-gate
.I file
.br
.B bertillon
.BR \-p | \-\-profile
.I file
.br
.B bertillon
.BR \-f | \-\-form
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B bertillon
.B \-\-emit\-table
.RI [ reference.tsv ]
.br
.B bertillon
.B \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bertillon
is given a hash, or a file of them, and reports
.I which registered hash types could have produced that value,
grouped by how expensive each is to test.
.PP
.B bertillon
is
.BR hashpipe (1)
under another name: one binary, reached through a symlink and dispatching on
.BR argv [0].
The same hash types and the same measured speeds are behind both.
.BR "hashpipe \-Z" " " .\|.\|.
is the same entry point for an installation that carries no symlink.
.PP
The option letters below are
.BR bertillon 's
own and have nothing to do with
.BR hashpipe 's:
.B \-t
is a cost tier, not a thread count, and
.B \-h
is help.
A value may be given in full or by its first letter, because the five emit
formats and the three tiers each have distinct initials, so
.PP
.RS
.nf
bertillon \-t e \-e h list.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
is the whole of
.BR "\-\-tier easy \-\-emit hashcat" .
.SH "WHAT IT DOES NOT DO"
The refusals are the point of the tool, and each is printed on the run
rather than left in this page.
.IP \(bu 3
.B "It does not identify a hash."
For a bare 32-character hex value the answer is 189 types, and nothing
about the value narrows it further.  MD5, MD5(SHA1(pass)) and MD5 iterated
twice all produce 32 hex characters.  Only computing separates them.
.IP \(bu 3
.B "It does not rank by likelihood."
Types come back cheapest first.  Cost is measured; likelihood is not
estimated at all.
.IP \(bu 3
.B "A tier is a budget, not a verdict."
Types outside the selected tier are untested, not ruled out, and the count
not tried is printed on every run.
.IP \(bu 3
.B "A verified pair is not a cracked-password record."
The output of a verification is a type identification.  Found-lines are the
business of
.BR hashpipe (1)
and
.BR mdxfind (1),
which own that format.
.IP \(bu 3
.B "When several types verify, all of them are genuine."
Nothing in the value chooses between them.  See
.BR "SEVERAL TYPES VERIFYING" .
.SH "READING A TARGET"
A
.I target
is either a
.I value
\(em a hash,
.IR hash : salt ,
or
.IR hash : salt : plaintext
\(em or a
.I file
containing such values one per line.
.PP
How many colon-separated fields the value has decides how it is read.  Two
fields could be
.IR hash : salt
or
.IR hash : plaintext
and nothing in the line says which, so both readings are tried and each is
labelled in the output.
.PP
When a plaintext is present and some registered type reproduces the hash,
that verification is reported and no candidate set is printed.  Verifying is
cheaper than reducing and it is a stronger answer.
.SS "A file target"
A file is read in full.  Up to 4601 lines are kept as a uniform sample of
the whole file, taken in one pass, so that a handful of damaged lines among
millions cannot move the answer.  The sampled lines are grouped, the
commonest group is the one reduced, and the line count, the sample size and
the number of distinct groups are printed.  When more than one group is
present the run says how many are not covered.
.PP
Lines are grouped by
.I field count
and by the
.I length of the first field.
Whether that field is hexadecimal is not part of the grouping.  A file whose
lines are all 32 characters long, half of them hex and half not, therefore
reports one group, and which of the two the answer describes follows from
whichever of them appears first in the sample.  Check with
.B \-f
when a list may not be uniform.
.SS "Standard input"
With no target,
.B bertillon
reads standard input and treats
.I every line
as a separate target: no sampling, no commonest-group reduction, and one
summary and one selection per line.  This is not the same operation as
naming the file, and on a list of any size it is not the one wanted.
.SS "A target that is neither"
A target naming no readable file is treated as a hash value.  If it also
contains a path separator,
.B bertillon
says so and carries on; the exit status is still 0.  That warning is the
only sign that a mistyped filename has been reduced as though it were a
hash.
.SH "COST TIERS"
Every registered type is placed in one of three tiers \(em
.BR easy ,
.B medium
and
.B hard
\(em by how expensive it is to test.  Tiers are cumulative:
.B \-\-tier hard
includes easy and medium unless
.B \-\-only
is given.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-table ,
the costing is approximate, and the run says so: types that combine their
salt from a pair are costed as though they did not, so an
.IR n -entry
salt file costs
.I n
squared rather than
.IR n .
Supplying the reference table measures them.  On a file of 1000
.IR hash : salt
lines the easy tier falls from 66 types to 65 when the table is supplied,
because e408 MD5DSALT moves out of easy and into hard.  Nothing is lost:
the type is still reachable, at the tier that reflects what it costs.
.PP
The reference path is always explicit.  There is no default location and no
search path, because a tool that silently reads an unexpected file changes
behaviour invisibly.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS "Selecting and widening"
.TP
.BI \-t " tier\fR, " \-\-tier " tier"
.BR easy ,
.B medium
or
.BR hard ,
or the first letter of one.  Default
.BR easy .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-only
Report that tier alone instead of it and everything cheaper.
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-truncation
Also include types whose digest is
.I longer
than the value, which apply if these values have been cut short.  Off by
default; the count it would add is reported on every run that has one.
.TP
.BI \-b " reference.tsv\fR, " \-\-table " reference.tsv"
Take the measured cost columns from this table rather than assuming.  See
.BR "COST TIERS" .
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-reduce
Report the candidate set.  This is what runs when no other mode is asked
for, so it never has to be typed.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Print the breakdown behind the two-line summary: how many types the negative
result would cover, and how many were not tried for each distinct reason.
.SS "Choosing what is printed"
.TP
.BI \-e " format\fR, " \-\-emit " format"
What to print, in full or by first letter.
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.B enum
\(em type numbers, in the form
.B "mdxfind \-M"
takes.  The default.
.IP \(bu 3
.B mdx
\(em a ready
.B "mdxfind \-h"
selection argument.
.IP \(bu 3
.B hashcat
\(em
.B "hashcat \-m"
modes, one per line, because
.B \-m
takes exactly one mode per run where
.B "mdxfind \-M"
takes a set.  The same answer is one argument for one tool and a sequence of
runs for the other.
.IP \(bu 3
.B john
\(em John format names, one per line.  Only portable names are emitted;
config-defined dynamics are numbered per machine and are not safe to name.
.IP \(bu 3
.B cmd
\(em a runnable
.B mdxfind
command line.
.RE
.IP
.BR hashcat (1)
and John name far fewer constructions than
.BR mdxfind (1)
does, so neither format can express every type in a selection.  The count
each drops is reported.  Covering what is printed does not cover the
reduction.
.SS "Other modes"
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-gate " " \fIfile\fR
Verify
.IR hash : plaintext
and
.IR hash : salt : plaintext
lines against every registered type, naming the types that reproduce the
hash.  The cost limit
.RB ( "hashpipe \-L" )
is computed from each line's own parsed work factor before the run, because
.B hashpipe
reports a cost-skipped type and an unhandled format with the same message
and the two need different next actions.  With the limit raised first, a
report that nothing handled a format can be believed.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-profile " " \fIfile\fR
Report what the list says collectively and a single line cannot: damage in
the file, whether an odd field count is real trouble or just salts that
contain a colon, whether a salt has been folded into the password field, and
how far the salts are reused.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-form " [" \fIfile\fR ]
For each input line, is it a hash at all and does it name its own format.
Each distinct answer is printed once with its count, commonest first, to
standard output; the totals go to standard error.  No type lists, no
verification.  Reads standard input when no file is given.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-per\-line
One line of output per line of input, in input order, without counts.
.TP
.B \-\-summary
The totals alone.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-emit\-table " \fR[" reference.tsv ]
Measure every type this binary knows and write the table.  With a
.IR reference ,
compare against it and stay silent when nothing moved; without one, write it
unconditionally.  See
.BR "EXIT STATUS" .
.RS
.PP
The
.BR salt_ceiling ,
.BR salt_rel ,
.BR field2_is_salt ,
.B n_acceptors
and
.B collides_with
columns are copied from the reference unchanged; this binary does not
measure them.
.B tools/bertillon\-probe
does, and takes about 35 minutes.
.PP
Types loaded from your own
.B userdef.txt
are not listed, because they depend on your setup rather than on this build.
.B \-\-include\-userdef
adds them with their measured columns left blank, and warns that the result
is not a table to commit.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage to standard output and exit 0.
.B \-h
is help here, and not the type-selecting regex it is in
.BR mdxfind (1).
.SH OUTPUT
The selection goes to standard output and everything else to standard
error, so the selection pipes.
.PP
With a single target the selection is the bare list.  A two-field value is
reduced under both readings, and each line of output is then prefixed by its
reading \(em the label
.B hash:salt
and a tab \(em so the two can be told apart downstream.  With more than one
target each line is prefixed instead by the target it answers, followed by a
tab.  Both appear in the examples below.
.PP
A verification prints one line per type that reproduced the hash:
.PP
.RS
.nf
VERIFIED	e1	MD5
.fi
.RE
.PP
That is an identification \(em type number and name \(em and deliberately
not a found line.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-gate
a line that verified is printed with the type name in front of it, and a
line that did not is echoed back on its own.  Three causes produce a bare
line and the output does not tell them apart: the password is wrong for that
hash, the fields are in the wrong order, or the construction is not
registered in this build.  Work through them in that order.  For the last,
an
.BR hx (1)
expression and a
.B userdef
entry add the construction; a wider search will not find it.
.SH "SEVERAL TYPES VERIFYING"
More than one registered type can reproduce the same hash from the same
input, and when that happens every one of them is reported.  None is wrong.
AICH over a single small block
.I is
SHA-1, so a 40-character hex value and its plaintext verify as SHA1, AICH
and SHA1UTF7 together.
.BR hashpipe (1)
reports only the first, because a cracker needs one answer;
.B bertillon
reports all of them because a reader has to choose.
.PP
Choosing is yours, and it is made with knowledge of where the list came
from, not from the value.  Neither is a verified line proof that the fields
are the right way round: exchanging the salt and the password in a line that
verified can verify again, as a different type, and both readings are true
statements about the bytes.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Normal completion.  This includes a target that named no readable file and
was read as a hash value instead.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: an unknown option, an unrecognised
.B \-t
or
.B \-e
value, more than 256 targets, a
.BR \-g ,
.B \-p
or
.B \-f
file that cannot be read, or no target with nothing on standard input, which
prints the usage.
.PP
Under
.B \-\-emit\-table
with a reference the status reports the comparison:
.TP
.B 0
compared, identical; the row count goes to standard error.
.TP
.B 1
compared, differs; the new table goes to standard output and the diagnosis
to standard error.
.TP
.B 2
no comparison was possible \(em the reference is absent, unreadable or
malformed.  This is never conflated with "nothing moved".
.SH EXAMPLES
A list of
.IR hash : salt
lines that nothing has solved.  The selection is suppressed here to show the
summary alone; it is what would have gone down the pipe:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon salted.txt 2>&1 >/dev/null
bertillon: salted.txt \-\- 1000 lines, 1000 sampled, 1 different form

bertillon: 66 types to try (32\-char hex + salt, easy tier).
           widen: \-\-tier medium +12, \-\-truncation +531.  962 not tried, \-v for why.
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B "\-\-tier hard"
is not offered on that ladder because it would add nothing to a value of
this form; only rungs that actually widen the set are shown.  A list of bare
values with no salt field reduces differently, and the difference is the
point \(em supplying a salt
.I cuts
the set, because a type that takes no salt cannot consume one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon unsalted.txt 2>&1 >/dev/null
bertillon: unsalted.txt \-\- 2000 lines, 2000 sampled, 1 different form

bertillon: 189 types to try (32\-char hex, easy tier).
           widen: \-\-tier medium +61, \-\-tier hard +1, \-\-truncation +531.  839 not tried, \-v for why.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The one type that the hard tier adds to that list, and nothing else:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-t hard \-o unsalted.txt
bertillon: unsalted.txt \-\- 2000 lines, 2000 sampled, 1 different form

bertillon: 1 type to try (32\-char hex, hard tier).
           widen: \-\-truncation +531.  1027 not tried, \-v for why.
e899
.fi
.RE
.PP
Two values on one command line, each answered on its own line and prefixed
by the target it answers.  The selection here is a ready
.B "mdxfind \-h"
argument rather than type numbers:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-t hard \-o \-e mdx 5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99 \\
      da39a3ee5e6b4b0d3255bfef95601890afd80709 2>/dev/null
5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99	\-h '^(LASTPASS)$'
da39a3ee5e6b4b0d3255bfef95601890afd80709	\-h '^(SHA1DRU|SAMSUNGSHA1)$'
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B \-v
gives the breakdown behind the two-line summary.  The four figures sum to
the not-tried total and each names a different kind of exclusion, which is
what makes a negative result mean anything: 66 types failing is evidence,
and 962 untested types are not.
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-v salted.txt 2>&1 >/dev/null
bertillon: derived digest lengths (bytes): [8, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 40, 48, 64]
bertillon: 1028 type(s), 22 with an uncertain parse
bertillon: salted.txt \-\- 1000 lines, 1000 sampled, 1 different form

bertillon: field 2 is not the plaintext \-\- 723 types tried, none matched.

bertillon: 66 types to try (32\-char hex + salt, easy tier).
           widen: \-\-tier medium +12, \-\-truncation +531.  962 not tried:

  a negative result covers these 66 types and no others.

  not tried:
      696  cannot produce a 32\-character hex form
           of those, 531 have a LONGER digest and would apply if these
           values have been cut short (\-\-truncation)
      251  take no salt, and one was supplied
       12  cost more than the easy tier allows
        3  emit the other letter case

  cost tiers are approximate: without \-\-table, types that
  combine salts in pairs are costed as if they did not.
  \-\-table hid\-shape.tsv supplies that.
.fi
.RE
.PP
"Cannot produce this form" is arithmetic and says nothing about any
algorithm.  "Take no salt" is about this input, and the same types apply to
a different one.  "Cost more than the tier allows" is deferred, not decided.
.PP
The same run with the measured table.  One type leaves the easy tier, the
approximation notice is gone, and a
.B "\-\-tier hard"
rung appears that was not on the ladder before:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-b hid\-shape.tsv salted.txt 2>&1 >/dev/null
bertillon: salted.txt \-\- 1000 lines, 1000 sampled, 1 different form

bertillon: 65 types to try (32\-char hex + salt, easy tier).
           widen: \-\-tier medium +12, \-\-tier hard +1, \-\-truncation +531.  963 not tried, \-v for why.
.fi
.RE
.PP
A value carrying its plaintext is answered outright:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon < solved.txt
VERIFIED	e1	MD5

bertillon: SOLVED as hash:plaintext \-\- 1 type(s) verified of 723 tried.
.fi
.RE
.PP
Three types reproducing one 40-character hex value:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon < collision.txt
VERIFIED	e8	SHA1
VERIFIED	e21	AICH
VERIFIED	e732	SHA1UTF7

bertillon: SOLVED as hash:plaintext \-\- 3 type(s) verified of 723 tried.
  3 types produce identical hash results for this input.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same on a three-field record, where the salt is explicit and three
different constructions still reproduce the hash:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon 'df9e335fc1cdd7c5c38824a15b1e0acd:ox5:000015'
VERIFIED	e31	MD5SALT
VERIFIED	e286	MD5MD5USER
VERIFIED	e517	MD5\-MD5PASS\-SALT

bertillon: SOLVED \-\- 3 type(s) verified of 413 tried. No candidate set.
  3 types produce identical hash results for this record.
.fi
.RE
.PP
A file of solved pairs.  If even a handful exist this identifies the list
outright and makes narrowing candidates unnecessary.  The first four lines
report the cost limit the run computed for itself from the bcrypt line:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-\-gate gate.txt
bertillon gate: 5 line(s), 1 carrying a work factor
bertillon gate: worst is bcrypt cost 12, est 0.17 s \-> \-L 10
bertillon gate: if hashpipe now reports that no registered type
bertillon gate: handled a format, the cost limit is not the reason.
MD5SALT df9e335fc1cdd7c5c38824a15b1e0acd:ox5:000015
MD5SALT b90b6311b4886c473f2c02d6b924bb08:ytp:00007584
MD5SALT c72c0bbd2f813eabfc6971693c01fcc9:1d2:)(*&^
MD5SALT 54a3aab52006ebd823b4917fd25927f9:a27:00001844
BCRYPT $2a$12$3xrp7EKdP0ggzfNg32UA2.nZjaC84YvQbcNBvT6/lC04oOimp8rSm:!!!!!!!!!
.fi
.RE
.PP
The first of those lines with the password replaced by a wrong one.  The
line comes back bare:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-\-gate g2.txt
bertillon gate: 1 line(s), 0 carrying a work factor
bertillon gate: no parsed work factor \-> \-L 10
bertillon gate: if hashpipe now reports that no registered type
bertillon gate: handled a format, the cost limit is not the reason.
df9e335fc1cdd7c5c38824a15b1e0acd:ox5:wrongpassword
.fi
.RE
.PP
The same line with the salt and the password exchanged.  It verifies again,
as a different type; the same hash and the same two values support both
readings, and only knowledge of the system the list came from says which one
describes it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-\-gate g3.txt
bertillon gate: 1 line(s), 0 carrying a work factor
bertillon gate: no parsed work factor \-> \-L 10
bertillon gate: if hashpipe now reports that no registered type
bertillon gate: handled a format, the cost limit is not the reason.
MD5\-MD5SALT\-PASSx01 df9e335fc1cdd7c5c38824a15b1e0acd:000015:ox5
.fi
.RE
.PP
What is in a file, distinct answers only, commonest first.  The rare lines
are the interesting ones \(em here, five truncated values that would
otherwise match nothing and look like a wordlist problem.  The totals that
would normally accompany this are on standard error and are discarded here:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-f mixed.txt 2>/dev/null
      220  digest|cipher? 16 bytes: digest length AND 16\-byte block multiple \-\- arithmetic cannot separate them
      100  digest         20 bytes, a digest length
       50  delimited      2 field(s), first 32 chars, sep ':'
        5  not\-hash       odd hex length 31 \-\- truncated or mis\-copied
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B digest|cipher?
is not indecision.  16 bytes is a real digest length
.I and
a whole number of cipher blocks, and arithmetic cannot choose between them.
.PP
Feeding hashcat.  The drop count is the part that matters:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-t e \-e h salted.txt
bertillon: salted.txt \-\- 1000 lines, 1000 sampled, 1 different form

bertillon: 66 types to try (32\-char hex + salt, easy tier).
           OF THOSE, 47 of 66 have no hashcat mode and are NOT in
           the list above. Covering what was printed does not cover
           the reduction.
           widen: \-\-tier medium +12, \-\-truncation +531.  962 not tried, \-v for why.
hash:salt	2611
2711
3610
2811
10
11
2630
20
21
24
11000
3800
21200
3710
3910
4110
4010
21300
4410
30
40
4430
33100
30500
23
.fi
.RE
.PP
That is 25 modes from the 19 of 66 types that have one, since a type can map
to several modes at once.  The count of modes therefore says nothing about
how much of the reduction is covered: 25 looks like more than 19, and 47
constructions are still untried.  The only way to try those is a tool that
can name them.
.PP
What the list as a whole says.  One salt on every record is the strongest
evidence available at scale: a hash taken over one concatenated string does
not record where the salt ended and the password began, so a single line has
as many readings as it has character positions and nothing to choose between
them.  One salt on every record chooses:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ ./bertillon \-p sitewide.txt
sitewide.txt \-\- 100 lines

  INTAKE
    no line\-ending, padding or escaping damage found

  FIELD COUNT
    most common     : 2 field(s) in 100 of 100 lines (100.00%)
    salt width      : 23
    expected        : 78.40% of lines would have 2 fields if the
                      salts were random printable text of that length
    difference      : +21.60 points
    \-> MORE conforming lines than that would predict, so the salts
       are not random printable text: a fixed salt, or one drawn
       from a restricted set such as hex, never contains a colon.
       The prediction does not apply here; nothing is wrong.

  FIELD BOUNDARY
    every last field ends or begins with the same 23 bytes:
      xKq7%$tR_2026_siteWide!
    \-> That string is on EVERY record, so it is not part of anyone's
       password: it is a salt that has been folded into the password
       field. These records are filed under the wrong split. They
       will still verify, which is why verifying cannot catch it.

  SALT REUSE
    records         : 100 (those whose salt is the usual 23 bytes)
    distinct salts  : 1
    most common     : 100 record(s), 100.0000%
      xKq7%$tR_2026_siteWide!
    \-> ONE SALT FOR THE WHOLE SITE. Every record shares it, so the
       boundary between salt and password is known, and that single
       fact settles a reading the individual lines cannot. Say that
       this is what decided it: "it verifies" does not decide
       anything, because the reading you are replacing verified too.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The INTAKE block also reports CRLF line endings, padding and escaping
damage, with the count for each.  A list with CRLF endings read against an
LF wordlist matches nothing at all, for the whole file, and looks exactly
like the wrong wordlist.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 3
A candidate set is not an identification, and no ordering within it carries
any claim about which type is more likely.  See
.BR "WHAT IT DOES NOT DO" .
.IP \(bu 3
Without
.BR \-\-table ,
cost tiers are approximate and every run says so.  Only one expansion class
has been measured: MD5DSALT composes its salt from a pair, so an
.IR n -entry
salt file costs
.I n
squared.  Every other type is assumed linear.
.IP \(bu 3
Lines in a file are grouped by field count and first-field length only.  A
list mixing hex and non-hex values of the same length reports one group and
answers for whichever appears first.  See
.BR "READING A TARGET" .
.IP \(bu 3
Reading from standard input answers every line separately and does not
reduce the file as a whole.  Name the file as a target to get that.
.IP \(bu 3
The candidate set is derived from the form of the value.  A value that names
its own format, such as a
.B $2a$
bcrypt hash, is not matched on its tag in this path: it reduces as a
60-character non-hex value and BCRYPT appears only at
.BR "\-\-tier hard" ,
where it belongs on cost.
.B \-f
is what reports the tag, and
.B \-\-gate
is what confirms it.
.IP \(bu 3
.B \-\-emit\-table
does not measure the
.BR salt_ceiling ,
.BR salt_rel ,
.BR field2_is_salt ,
.B n_acceptors
and
.B collides_with
columns; they are copied from the reference.
.IP \(bu 3
There is no
.BR \-V .
The version is
.BR "hashpipe \-V" ,
this page having been written against 1.203.
.IP \(bu 3
The
.B bertillon
name has to be reachable.  An installed
.B hashpipe
older than this work carries neither the symlink nor
.BR \-Z ,
and the tool then resolves only from the build tree.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B bertillon
A symlink to the
.B hashpipe
binary.  The tool dispatches on
.BR argv [0];
.B "hashpipe \-Z"
is the same entry point under the original name.
.TP
.B hid\-shape.tsv
The measured reference table, as written by
.BR \-\-emit\-table .
There is no default location: the path is given with
.B \-\-table
or it is not used.
.TP
.B userdef.txt
User-defined types, read only when
.B MDXFIND_CACHE
is set.  These are excluded from
.B \-\-emit\-table
unless
.B \-\-include\-userdef
is given.
